#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133884 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133700 :question/id #uuid "3ff94d55-a99a-5de6-bc84-410d03f18a28" :question/math-academy-id "e-9086" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0), (1,1)$ and $(-1,1),$ traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}x\\sin{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+ (x^2\\cos{y}-y^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}y}$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2f/2fa3c84359cec5f5e0750552e5a4b24b20b8dbc3d65b909aea908e1505cfb5e3.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)= x\\sin{y}$ and $Q(x,y)= x^2\\cos{y}-y^2.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y) :  -y\\leq x\\leq y,\\, 0\\leq y \\leq 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dx+({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48652211-e469-55b9-96cb-2526a4135d34" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997 :topic/math-academy-id 2151 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Type I Regions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050264 :topic/math-academy-id 2152 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Type II Regions"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134068 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24768801-8fab-53fd-a0cd-39980d484a21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241496} {:answer/id #uuid "431267f1-e896-500c-84d8-2cf2426b2403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241781} {:answer/id #uuid "991fc2be-8a77-504e-b717-422b1852dda6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242518} {:answer/id #uuid "9fedb320-619d-5fd4-bb97-8324e0b2afd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242580} {:answer/id #uuid "b8be4494-9828-596a-a764-bd23250e5578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}2x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24768801-8fab-53fd-a0cd-39980d484a21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241496} :answer-field/id #uuid "f91572ca-da1d-5596-9c9c-7fe17a660ab0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243874}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "700fbfa1-78ed-5b39-a539-ef3e4dd8874d" :question/math-academy-id "q-108467" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(1,0)$ and $(1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2e63a57e50b60d7f0c6a3ce12d164716e2cba71c6bd4c6df10460a35beba4209.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 2x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03eefc0e-dc5f-50f8-8aa8-37fedc5b6795" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}-3y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415777} {:answer/id #uuid "4dfb517d-314f-5d0e-adde-8ffca8af7876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2x-3)\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415796} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a94d74-4321-5c41-9daa-93ace7652c67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x(3y+2)\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415835} {:answer/id #uuid "e3efbf16-6539-5ad4-bd13-0040d7d457e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x(2y-3)\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415838} {:answer/id #uuid "eb384a4b-9a06-5ed5-bfb1-8be217c6cefa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(3y-2)\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3efbf16-6539-5ad4-bd13-0040d7d457e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x(2y-3)\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415838} :answer-field/id #uuid "9db11add-d8be-5410-9153-1e6c3098e557" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415851}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1939a88a-677c-5293-8524-d0ecfaa79350" :question/math-academy-id "q-42085" :question/problem "Consider the square $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,3)$ and $(-1,3),$ traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨3xy,{x}^{2}y⟩,$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/6f/6f831b2b707e63e8eb34ff5f6c1c45d0267f51001de2560834d212506b48ee41.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}3xy\\,dx+{x}^{2}y\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3xy$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}3xy\\,dx+{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy-3x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x(2y-3)\\text{ }dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c64f97f-3cc5-550d-9b53-b55d5beece33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}({e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241286} {:answer/id #uuid "10fe1edc-f3c0-53d9-ba62-f20627efd921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241328} {:answer/id #uuid "6a3eb839-93ab-5ade-a75d-b2a6f9373bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}(2x{e}^{y}-1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242135} {:answer/id #uuid "c510257b-72ce-50c4-8778-b5bdef960fb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{3y-{y}^{2}}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242916} {:answer/id #uuid "dca979b3-434a-561b-8d4a-39e12feb7842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dca979b3-434a-561b-8d4a-39e12feb7842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243143} :answer-field/id #uuid "f269e409-7bf4-5c2e-903d-d263915d4aba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243857}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "806c47fd-68e8-5b88-97f5-76f2521ac858" :question/math-academy-id "q-109249" :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=y$ and $x=4y-{y}^{2}.$ Express ${∮}_{C}({e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dx+(2x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/23/239867aec8cff7840667d2f4eafa9bc97d4fc5af09b684d863fef06fd8fd28ad.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=2x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2{e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):y\\le x\\le 4y-{y}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dx+(2x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244257 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c6fb1e6-13c2-5304-9a0b-fd9f38873188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242152} {:answer/id #uuid "8855dd4e-cf52-5421-a738-17951cb4daa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{x+2}^{{x}^{2}+x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242377} {:answer/id #uuid "c441ba1b-5791-5a9d-88bf-8622cc73ba19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242905} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6ea074-1c33-5438-b183-744a1d23db35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242975} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d53892-c143-515e-b312-92905c82dc37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}(2y-x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c6fb1e6-13c2-5304-9a0b-fd9f38873188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242152} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a6164af-b887-57b9-91dd-6c4ddd92fdb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243488}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a5b7043e-fa21-5314-98d6-598e5c27911d" :question/math-academy-id "q-109263" :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=x+2.$ Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2},{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}⟩,$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/e3/e32b8e7d6ce80d7cedad928f9a2f7acc3c3d7f06add7909e856db27d154068d6.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "003753be-91cd-5401-b185-1a2caa7b0ba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}x\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323978} {:answer/id #uuid "11403fb2-cc40-5faf-bc1a-820a3836d9e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(x+y)dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324129} {:answer/id #uuid "14390247-b8d4-5256-ae63-0dceb79e5d7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324154} {:answer/id #uuid "acbb3d66-c472-5d9a-9faa-95357fdcbd7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}(x-y)dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325437} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e9f50f-53fb-5ee2-baa4-64890b3e25c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6e9f50f-53fb-5ee2-baa4-64890b3e25c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326148} :answer-field/id #uuid "c166fb2a-6179-53b7-8884-9c52889f51d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326570}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dfeb76a7-b3b4-5da5-8979-e2b8f3b5d4af" :question/math-academy-id "q-41702" :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the $x$-axis, for $\\pi \\le x\\le 2\\pi .$ Express ${\\int }_{C}xdx-(x-y)dy$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/26/264cb888010525848f647669653d34383535dd32e6ac264927105540c3b85f9f.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x$ and $Q(x,y)=-(x-y).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):\\pi \\le x\\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the Green's Theorem we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xdx-(x-y)dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326928 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "019501e8-6ea6-5368-99e8-cde9c215f7d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323987} {:answer/id #uuid "31bcb739-40d2-561c-b2d4-44bd167631a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y}(1+x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324375} {:answer/id #uuid "6f3759c8-4733-51a4-abea-b8fa6dcbec97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1+x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324885} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f9d8ec-7102-5b9e-a9d0-b789d1dc61fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}(1+x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325569} {:answer/id #uuid "de42923d-c758-5be4-a046-273bd9c961bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1+x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f3759c8-4733-51a4-abea-b8fa6dcbec97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1+x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324885} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e66e1ab-6bec-5d89-bae1-cf35e17f31ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326348}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "977cc01a-2db6-51fc-a3f5-772fb936cc60" :question/math-academy-id "q-42107" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(0,-1)$ and $(1,0),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y⟩,$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/6d/6d4f13abad5cc193dccedee3114fbd9cbd9fb8e6b5b3ddb0d07f0ed2ed51f4ff.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le y+1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1+x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19d912c2-2ff0-503a-91db-848ccbab9dca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19d912c2-2ff0-503a-91db-848ccbab9dca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362507} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9a9ee48-7978-5cd0-a274-d255560a0858" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362506}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "076b553d-9b94-5414-af96-ed5daed9cd1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-168558" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to write down a double integral that gives the circulation of $F$ along $C,$ transversed counterclockwise.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/46/4693916207540a6453c6c3775824ba577e62fea0a18b07e96f8814901d9ad80e.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\,dx+(-2xy)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=-2xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2y-(-4y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2y\\,dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e823e3a1-e75b-5988-a51e-22e31b8a9028" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386392} {:answer/id #uuid "fcde3246-eebb-595d-9c9f-b55275d0c8a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386393} {:answer/id #uuid "8f60dcb7-b93b-52d6-ab98-048f7b9af687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386394} {:answer/id #uuid "c8832ecf-0003-515c-96de-a76fba6e0484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386395} {:answer/id #uuid "76ced2c3-93fa-57fb-804a-79e1c5bb7e94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76ced2c3-93fa-57fb-804a-79e1c5bb7e94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386396} :answer-field/id #uuid "375112e1-4816-53a5-b797-a9ab4149ddab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386391}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec0b1748-e212-5d96-8358-0b705e724a9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109254" :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the unit circle and the coordinate axes in the first quadrant. Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,x-{e}^{-{y}^{2}}⟩,$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/e4/e48034aed1f41cf1f9b30c057186879233fe05cd2ba720dcee436398a732b6ce.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx+(x-{e}^{-{y}^{2}})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=x-{e}^{-{y}^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx+(x-{e}^{-{y}^{2}})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386397 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c0ab6a5-fedc-5172-9726-7c8b92e6d238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386399} {:answer/id #uuid "269ba7b5-dbe7-55bd-823b-41e9e736d2f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-2x+2}(y+4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386400} {:answer/id #uuid "70a164ad-574b-5019-8963-919944f5cbe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386401} {:answer/id #uuid "985a602a-628b-5bc3-b3e1-54bc6f12da7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{x+1}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386402} {:answer/id #uuid "215ea562-e0fc-5144-bcf0-a8d1cb3bb25f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-2x+2}(x-(2+{y}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70a164ad-574b-5019-8963-919944f5cbe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386401} :answer-field/id #uuid "68b205c3-aadd-5002-89e3-15ef39ecf484" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386398}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d02b88c9-898d-50c4-a59e-b0f309ffc0a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-109291" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,-1),(1,0)$ and $(0,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x⟩,$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/c2/c2e08c233cb31124234ab6e5179998a3bdc148024632c7770bdea46cd37dff83.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx+(xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y+2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x-1\\le y\\le -2x+2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx+(xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415860 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "268fff15-9ff4-545d-b493-583e83e9bc8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415784} {:answer/id #uuid "97cc6eca-3853-56fc-b7f2-30027a479f07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415817} {:answer/id #uuid "9947a74c-b42b-54c1-b91a-071c2ba392f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2}^{1+x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415818} {:answer/id #uuid "bb803066-80a1-53e9-86c1-b29e905214f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2}^{1+x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415825} {:answer/id #uuid "f479d213-1c2b-52df-b690-7c60433780fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f479d213-1c2b-52df-b690-7c60433780fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415841} :answer-field/id #uuid "546404cf-f1c6-53f4-be84-033edffb255a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415847}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0fd75856-9255-5679-88e6-6aeda24c9a6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109288" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(1,0)$ and $(-1,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}({y}^{2}-2xy)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2})\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/eac7a442f0a794db0606b95aa722320d099f2bcc4e530fc6b62e9be889335da2.png)\n\n \n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}-2xy$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y-2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}-2xy)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2x-(2y-2x)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415861 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ea792f4-c1dd-5faa-a83b-af57c9e0e101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415792} {:answer/id #uuid "89af553e-248b-5c72-ab62-1d4e4315eda8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415812} {:answer/id #uuid "d8db4f17-8974-53ec-bf38-95643619fcaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415836} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b0ae0b-92be-5756-b5b7-435352928f96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415837} {:answer/id #uuid "f8bad17e-7a80-55fa-877b-8cf3e386ec15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ea792f4-c1dd-5faa-a83b-af57c9e0e101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415792} :answer-field/id #uuid "55995149-0733-5aaa-8feb-5219bcb91ca4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415848}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "196f72ff-1ff2-5e8e-8905-6141cb15ad66" :question/math-academy-id "q-109248" :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the $y$-axis, for $x\\ge 0.$ Express ${∮}_{C}(x{y}^{2}-{e}^{x})\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-1)\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/92/92b9a6c83dd18a5599bbee71d714b26fddd62eb32917a5df983edc232664067b.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}},\\,-1\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(x{y}^{2}-{e}^{x})\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415870 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05110615-25ea-51a7-9ec6-fe0602b9d168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(y+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415778} {:answer/id #uuid "1f28acf4-a8d0-5ea3-bf43-bdd1494c0d0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415782} {:answer/id #uuid "50d8a1be-eef6-58bc-8c04-9f0c7278b337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415797} {:answer/id #uuid "6622f567-4548-58c5-9664-16e371447a8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415804} {:answer/id #uuid "d3af5033-176c-54c5-aa13-e691e816dd84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6622f567-4548-58c5-9664-16e371447a8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415804} :answer-field/id #uuid "0551862e-7b6d-55d9-92c8-f74ab0aa1c10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415846}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c14fef09-0118-528c-b9c9-91bf74ebf7eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-109245" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-2,0),(1,0),(1,1)$ and $(-2,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x{y}^{2})\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/a5/a5404053d83188af931c19350d5b6e42145009cf7f67b9dffcc08edc16ad3627.png)\n\n \n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-2\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420413 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ebea952-0566-5c92-8d0e-e040e7712ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420407} {:answer/id #uuid "3f01f0a5-e9c8-5d76-9040-7592e7edc559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}-1}^{1-{x}^{2}}2(x+y)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420408} {:answer/id #uuid "ad95bd1a-50e9-550a-940a-b1d62f28413d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}(x+y)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420409} {:answer/id #uuid "c5b67534-974d-530a-a5f5-af0136eb1850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{0}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420410} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc4f4e1-426a-5f5a-951d-5f244fa030ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ebea952-0566-5c92-8d0e-e040e7712ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420407} :answer-field/id #uuid "25ac563f-3cb9-5a93-a3a0-eb472a59f7c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420412}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "426ff1c4-32bc-566d-87df-d5bec20e08eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-42074" :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and $y=0.$ Express ${∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-y)\\,dx+(2x-{y}^{2})\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/a3/a3fe0bbc283a2eaacffad4b6be071f601781b6b77b2df0dd9b30abb22653c738.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$ and $Q(x,y)=2x-{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1-{x}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-y)\\,dx+(2x-{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-(-1))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral as a Repeated Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133886 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133702 :question/id #uuid "d6e8fac3-5dfc-5487-bd81-dc33aeae5f1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9097" :question/problem "![](images/83/83bd636f3a294c641e171c5a8281aaa7068e1931a06229064314e8f96efd0c3a.png)\n\nCalculate ![](images/c4/c4d83a5e5d029ef5f5e06b68d3cc39ea72ecf0c404f18d6a0ded750b0e2a6f39.svg) where ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) be the region bounded by ![](images/27/278f99df1e14fe0ebfb61bdb72262a62670dcaa60d322148bc3ee591b278ec2d.svg) If ![](images/1a/1aaddf0d4be4d873ff6c0600b0b239e0565a05ca8daaff7ca92bd5c70bebcf03.svg) and ![](images/4b/4be111afe7c23428ae5024a8bb9424de4d5b9e8cce74d2258fdffb9bdca9bcfd.svg) have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing ![](images/87/87e7e0743ecd6609730f4b9a9b3681b1f360ca86bcb209e03561a197b18782f3.svg) then Green's theorem states that\n![](images/ac/accf185e064f4c9c99cc6ad1437aabc7d5471cc332ff6c38d51a3f11ec6e26f2.svg)\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that ![](images/09/090c7fe781ec77412a904bb44dda28812c8a4e9a8524886e36041ffcabe9f0a4.svg) and ![](images/d7/d719ec6c4bb88290d50d81fc268cc438cb3002659b6ac79210889d779a09d44c.svg) So,\n\n![](images/94/94e9af3cff89c9bcf412776abcab67d1852ba083401829edcd746cb19311c8b9.svg)\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n![](images/ec/ec5ca2469ae1fd23385b27e906cb952f6b07c2bbfe02acbc42afa20284d4a122.svg)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c67da0d8-518b-5fdb-b6ba-6c1c95c22d9b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050567 :topic/math-academy-id 2198 :topic/title "Using Green's Theorem to Calculate Area"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134097 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074f2b27-d82e-5431-893f-5cb420b7db07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130739} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb000df-3f30-5d92-833d-b570ce4d2fc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130740} {:answer/id #uuid "67a5cc30-ac62-56fb-b984-6bf8c55a68e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130741} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1f5867-3adf-5ebd-8085-208cd0303ff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130742} {:answer/id #uuid "6da1aafe-3858-50f0-8369-80cd33a8831e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130743}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3d6f2a4f-8215-5cfe-aa3b-409cfed8d343" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130738}] :question/id #uuid "5d511ff2-5d88-51e6-9da4-d8015a261af1" :question/math-academy-id "q-126515" :question/problem "![](images/32/320a45751b2e718c66bb721cfcd78a2d206f08a9998fbbffddb1581ee995cf34.png)\n\nCalculate $∮_(∂D)xdy - ydx$, where $D$ is the region shown above."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8672bfbf-4d4d-5253-9f83-f75552aab637" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130757} {:answer/id #uuid "e05949f9-9eb7-5677-b997-22c6339b8950" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130758} {:answer/id #uuid "ffdc5cdf-d5f3-58a1-ae3b-d8584e3667be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130759} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf2c260-85c4-50d2-8cbc-b06eda27ff5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130760} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7aa802-5a2e-5905-9460-5989c7b7829e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130761}] :answer-field/id #uuid "49e8a026-4e1e-5ba9-b726-87255f49ad4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130756} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d09c91eb-6025-563c-9e1e-422c46c45a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369381} {:answer/id #uuid "f7db36f3-e726-57a8-990c-cb139c3421dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369382} {:answer/id #uuid "0d9cd067-f29c-5dd2-92a7-39171feb6a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369383} {:answer/id #uuid "5df13d18-ed06-5111-b470-86ff1f65bc05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369384} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab6388d-f385-5685-97ba-bf84a736e56a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d9cd067-f29c-5dd2-92a7-39171feb6a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369383} :answer-field/id #uuid "e35c2b8b-a5ba-5e72-9086-b121a2efaac4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369380}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54b4afaf-80a9-50b2-aaaa-863d5d133db6" :question/math-academy-id "q-126578" :question/problem "![](images/52/52815b087f869769950d37ab6b95edb75accf030e742ed16b4679b6a41d7d638.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}dx+x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=1$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\pi -\\pi  \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fe866fa-6339-5f10-8ce3-3d5938c3785e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369436} {:answer/id #uuid "0aafa5fa-6806-5c1a-bd6e-a8f0ec680d0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369437} {:answer/id #uuid "b169f03c-00d2-5df3-887b-9ea86b4a1f34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369438} {:answer/id #uuid "2e03cd88-a4dd-5d49-b397-096aeb5b4219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369439} {:answer/id #uuid "8e91bdb9-02ae-5c29-aa67-a875b2bc2112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e03cd88-a4dd-5d49-b397-096aeb5b4219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369439} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2ee2d3e-bbe0-5737-8885-1738f355a09b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369435}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "32a18b85-d978-5b18-8fff-914bdccf82dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-126853" :question/problem "![](images/39/393bed476f2e8d84fc7e5dc721d77749f7f259834e03a94a48a08533ae9befa6.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}2y\\,dx+4x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2y$ and $Q(x,y)=4x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}2y\\,dx+4x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4-2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (6\\cdot 4-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (24-4) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369441 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "992dce16-160e-53f6-8630-c0af64cae1cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369443} {:answer/id #uuid "2bfd9130-7a02-5b45-a0d5-85f79505dd79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369444} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce07f1c-821c-51aa-8553-62af6bab118d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369445} {:answer/id #uuid "905e612c-8067-5089-bf8b-dc0ca7481414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369446} {:answer/id #uuid "bae5a3fa-eb19-574d-b4eb-7f975d579bd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bfd9130-7a02-5b45-a0d5-85f79505dd79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369444} :answer-field/id #uuid "37fee2c5-b026-5dca-a84d-1a957d12aae4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369442}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7aa45530-2cd6-5788-83f4-81c4c36b6f4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-126856" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6b52e4f2f37d6ba6971fbc8f6e1d610ab7e0c9d11341ea3b6a6772bff8f40e37.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}3y\\,dx+2x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3y$ and $Q(x,y)=2x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}3y\\,dx+2x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-3)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =-(\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & =-(9\\pi -\\pi ) \\\\  & =-8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74b43056-e8dd-536d-b56a-12ac2800c12b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "364" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369457} {:answer/id #uuid "ca09836f-3c6e-5b5f-841d-68ede18363c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "215" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369458} {:answer/id #uuid "ac3caf47-5329-54b1-8c87-bcf0da3ef96b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "424" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369459} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b5b1dc-7bc7-5bb9-8b23-063bc496f471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "342" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369460} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2e77d0-1779-5c5c-9e64-d3cabd705415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74b43056-e8dd-536d-b56a-12ac2800c12b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "364" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369457} :answer-field/id #uuid "175c17d1-364f-5ab7-a6b1-2ce4de237b97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369456}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "017f8f34-6e37-562a-87cf-df5a06a7e434" :question/math-academy-id "q-126860" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5a42039e2501a3909062fd6ed92e0879df03f58fa85a62b5e738e8abc78b903.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}8x\\,dy-5y\\,dx,$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-5y$ and $Q(x,y)=8x.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-5,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}8x\\,dy-5y\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(8-(-5))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}13\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =13{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =13\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =13\\cdot ({8}^{2}-{6}^{2}) \\\\  & =13\\cdot (64-36) \\\\  & =13\\cdot 28 \\\\  & =364.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369497 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e6750b8-75e8-5604-bdf3-5b26e689ff79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369499} {:answer/id #uuid "71c2554d-1b8f-50ee-904e-bf572ea954dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369500} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d1e293-45b2-553f-8738-81af539d167d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369501} {:answer/id #uuid "808c516e-8fb0-5d9d-b3c3-71bd5e0c0d18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369502} {:answer/id #uuid "56bd1130-5922-503b-9779-186620077dd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3d1e293-45b2-553f-8738-81af539d167d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369501} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a4245ec-3053-53c4-bb04-e49df5e84b57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369498}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3c22304-91b2-51e6-94d5-71e3689e1379" :question/math-academy-id "q-126894" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e86380c457bc9d37d0180057d4ce8c035055986d32254d95fab3a69254531a5.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}6y\\,dx+9x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6y$ and $Q(x,y)=9x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(9-6)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3\\,\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 5\\pi  \\\\  & =15\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "220be106-05bc-5093-b00a-7db60bd8ecaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431288} {:answer/id #uuid "37256756-976d-5c9b-9621-726f21d7d740" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431295} {:answer/id #uuid "3add5703-5575-518a-8fb6-785fcc0ae348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431297} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c51c42-3a40-510d-8c2c-1c2f6b8e0682" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431327} {:answer/id #uuid "ddae96ae-32de-50a4-bd74-13c0f92807b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddae96ae-32de-50a4-bd74-13c0f92807b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431334} :answer-field/id #uuid "800a953b-6548-5c1f-a6b5-8f730ae36aed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431344}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e6cffb2-428d-5a69-9a22-1115ee05f052" :question/math-academy-id "q-126891" :question/problem "![](images/e7/e71bc835596d8699688f3623f339c26d365a8bb6e888742cbea10fc9da722adc.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}5y\\,dx+11x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=5y$ and $Q(x,y)=11x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=5,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(11-5)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {4}^{2}-\\frac{\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 14\\pi  \\\\  & =84\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0918efff-3716-5e79-bc16-ce01b647dee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431283} {:answer/id #uuid "0a4ab89c-0bd1-5d9d-bbfe-405c2f326073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431284} {:answer/id #uuid "b158c6b6-52a5-5218-9685-8e39e2b380c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431323} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f98841-106f-5d7f-bf0f-b27f11e0da85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431324} {:answer/id #uuid "fe684fde-4550-539f-a64b-9a955faeea4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1f98841-106f-5d7f-bf0f-b27f11e0da85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431324} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eb80ec8-d8a5-5f3b-808d-db46b94db516" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431339}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2fd9b28-2de8-59c7-8e8c-46e14a79f680" :question/math-academy-id "q-126880" :question/problem "![](images/65/65047f9bf4f035000175fc55ee3ba6eeb4aa7ec39674a1984b95ba98a3f754f6.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}8y\\,dx+13x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=8y$ and $Q(x,y)=13x.$ So,  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=8,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}8y\\,dx+13x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(13-8)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5\\,{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\frac{6\\cdot 6}{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (18-4) \\\\  & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133889 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133705 :question/id #uuid "3cbc5b4a-dde4-59b0-bc34-08c4d8776e0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9489" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(2x-y)^2\\,\\mathbf{i}+(3y-2z)^2\\,\\mathbf{j}+(x-2z)^2\\,\\mathbf{k}.$ Evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j} + R\\,\\mathbf k$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}=\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}+\\dfrac{\\partial R}{\\partial z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((2x-y)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((3y-2z)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x-2z)^{2}) \\\\  & =4(2x-y)+6(3y-2z)-4(x-2z) \\\\  & =4x+14y-4z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf { F}$ at the point $(1,2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(1,2,1) & =4(1)+14(2)-4(1) \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6b79409-5a5b-533b-8d46-4e0e6d963b3a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c79d74f-4409-57e4-b361-e941cc41aa8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3y{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407618} {:answer/id #uuid "941fb884-9dbd-5e6e-bdb3-9c13326a2f3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3y{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407620} {:answer/id #uuid "d9966256-2b26-5637-969d-ec652f55ff58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{xy}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{z}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407624} {:answer/id #uuid "ec0916bb-4bf2-5bfb-8625-f74c2a28d4bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{xy}\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407626} {:answer/id #uuid "eecb625e-fb38-58c5-8c7d-425f0374e2c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j+3y{z}^{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c79d74f-4409-57e4-b361-e941cc41aa8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3y{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407618} :answer-field/id #uuid "fafbd1ee-fb02-5785-b3f4-4426b308c48e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407630}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db7680f4-57f3-5a1a-a81a-52c92b75f829" :question/math-academy-id "q-105555" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,i-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j+y{z}^{3}\\,k.$ \nFind $∇\\cdot F.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3y{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134369 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f95316f-165c-5113-8ee9-c9367379b55b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324115} {:answer/id #uuid "23c7a9c5-c903-5481-b43f-909fffa5ca4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324265} {:answer/id #uuid "ae04b775-dac5-5210-bd8d-4c5a06b587a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325444} {:answer/id #uuid "bae12623-0161-5a6a-b882-9ee3f846c29e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325578} {:answer/id #uuid "ce705a3e-a8b9-5748-843b-3a1311fd41b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce705a3e-a8b9-5748-843b-3a1311fd41b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325765} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4fb8b77-4723-5632-b763-838f5f897e89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326580}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17dfa481-85bd-5863-b436-260f29cb7515" :question/math-academy-id "q-41839" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=xyz\\,i+{e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,j+({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi z+2xz)\\,k.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(1,0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi z+2xz) \\\\  & =yz-{e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x+\\pi {e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi z+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(1,0,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(1,0,0) & =(0)(0)-{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\pi {e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2(1) \\\\  & =0-(-1)+\\pi (1)+2 \\\\  & =1+\\pi +2 \\\\  & =\\pi +3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ec53b9b-3368-5896-a9e8-6eee1ee5ad72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ec53b9b-3368-5896-a9e8-6eee1ee5ad72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234643} :answer-field/id #uuid "a43e1a29-bf97-56d8-8130-064a3d0e5a3d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235173}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "965ed3cc-e6bf-5115-8afe-a7e9b6d2886f" :question/math-academy-id "q-168615" :question/problem "$\\text{div}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0+2x+0 \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259486 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40009387-47a9-502b-bd5f-f3dde55dcd0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡yz+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257356} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2fcf0d-78fd-5b9e-b1e2-2e872a328b4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258302} {:answer/id #uuid "cdc0147c-45f9-5254-a39e-6c04b28b8428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡yz+xz+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258563} {:answer/id #uuid "d2ed9a59-6327-5c52-a5f9-d81a53d8a6d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡yz+xyz+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258625} {:answer/id #uuid "e04475a1-0735-506c-9851-ac24e0742763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡yz+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e04475a1-0735-506c-9851-ac24e0742763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡yz+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258736} :answer-field/id #uuid