17592186264265} {:answer/id #uuid "bf08727f-4fff-542b-8537-f96fbde04c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264665} {:answer/id #uuid "d535bc6a-edfa-521d-bf62-0df28519ae30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d5cf691-6752-5dc4-9355-e5e32cd7af90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263381} :answer-field/id #uuid "836536a2-6d1f-5649-aacd-967f39893192" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265478}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "baf7ed03-0d49-58d2-ac15-c74b6a028957" :question/math-academy-id "q-131178" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i+y{x}^{2}\\,j+x{z}^{2}{e}^{y}\\,k.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(1,0,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{z}^{2}{e}^{y}) \\\\  & =z\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}+2xz{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(1,0,-2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(1,0,-2) & =(-2)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0+{1}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =-2+1-4 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e7210bc-4393-5891-b645-0416e337493e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263130} {:answer/id #uuid "57a5d39a-7a9e-5cfb-b9d8-3e21904353aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263756} {:answer/id #uuid "59856e9e-86de-5aec-8851-459d7aff7f97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263769} {:answer/id #uuid "763ec751-6041-53d3-bf83-ec80b69c1986" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264028} {:answer/id #uuid "e75e9fb8-16a8-58be-8ba5-2fa6ec19602d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e75e9fb8-16a8-58be-8ba5-2fa6ec19602d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265014} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ba2b1d0-6260-5b94-8683-7d316171b0b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265323}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0861a93-f1e8-5e67-91e3-502d85562273" :question/math-academy-id "q-131179" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=x(y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,i+xy{e}^{z}\\,j+z(x+1)\\,k.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(1,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x(y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z(x+1)) \\\\  & =(y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)+x{e}^{z}+(x+1) \\\\  & =y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x{e}^{z}+x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(1,1,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(1,1,0) & =1\\cdot {e}^{0}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1\\cdot {e}^{0}+1+1 \\\\  & =1+0+1+1+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362524 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "575f8899-a543-5ab8-8962-53939ce1cefe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "575f8899-a543-5ab8-8962-53939ce1cefe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362526} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e2b5331-a4e0-527d-b59c-5d4964d4f87a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362525}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82bf21ed-f804-5bd0-8b8b-a11ec7e6dab6" :question/math-academy-id "q-168624" :question/problem "$∇\\cdot G=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $G=P\\,\\text{i}+Q\\,\\text{j}+R\\,\\text{k}$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $G$ all exist, then the divergence of $G$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot G=\\text{div}\\,G=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot G & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(0)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(0)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-xy-2{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383041 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60937b06-70ea-5c5a-b823-3f7549956fb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383043} {:answer/id #uuid "7b44972d-191a-5729-ae14-a89b1f4b0c42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383044} {:answer/id #uuid "bc975208-2acd-5024-a13d-ca18e4d8b16f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383045} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3e76e5-a29e-5d75-b27c-e2d8dfd0de01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383046} {:answer/id #uuid "8e46db48-04cb-5171-923b-8c8d70b490f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e46db48-04cb-5171-923b-8c8d70b490f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383047} :answer-field/id #uuid "81a2e1fd-a1e3-5cc4-a1da-9e8f0ebbe408" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383042}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c8a3eea-616a-5c49-bb08-64624b7ee7d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-123192" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F={x}^{2}yz\\,i+x{y}^{2}z\\,j+xy{z}^{2}\\,k.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(1,2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =2xyz+2xyz+2xyz \\\\  & =6xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(1,2,2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(1,2,2) & =6(1)(2)(2) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1b52115-29e3-5e95-b508-73c86b6c7161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z{e}^{xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\frac{1}{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385417} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb2c780-ae80-5c02-98ec-088e06656270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z{e}^{xz}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385418} {:answer/id #uuid "c23d4ba1-b4af-5e1d-8241-74ca34e9132f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z{e}^{xz}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\frac{1}{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385419} {:answer/id #uuid "6137c52a-00ed-5c54-9df7-7714367d3018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xz{e}^{xz}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\frac{1}{yz}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385420} {:answer/id #uuid "c82109a8-b32c-5783-b93b-9700fb963ae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z{e}^{xz}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\frac{1}{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c82109a8-b32c-5783-b93b-9700fb963ae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z{e}^{xz}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\frac{1}{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385421} :answer-field/id #uuid "4373b52e-949a-57c9-b4c4-b3ece34fb1b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385416}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09702191-588f-54f0-a2ed-482de5d4de8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-41837" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨{e}^{xz}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡yz⟩.$ Find $\\text{div}\\,F.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xz}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(0)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡yz) \\\\  & =z{e}^{xz}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\frac{1}{z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407632 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e054f8-7842-51d9-944e-506f086c64ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407614} {:answer/id #uuid "30bd69d5-9aed-5f53-bd57-88c0d8b71e93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407615} {:answer/id #uuid "951b5e1a-06b7-5f54-b81b-2e7935528e41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407621} {:answer/id #uuid "b2537554-7ead-5776-b7ea-b0a5c6ea2d72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407622} {:answer/id #uuid "de576d13-a320-57ea-81fe-65cb1f522e77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08e054f8-7842-51d9-944e-506f086c64ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407614} :answer-field/id #uuid "55f12593-79dd-5e1b-8574-a974a94bfc5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407628}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f48c368c-417f-5ffe-9791-056b549ac545" :question/math-academy-id "q-105559" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,i+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y\\,j+{e}^{2z}\\,k.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(0,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{2z}) \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y+\\pi y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y+2{e}^{2z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(0,1,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(0,1,0) & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =0+0-\\pi +2 \\\\  & =2-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "193ddeed-a793-53c6-ba1e-92e68b0f73f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424198} {:answer/id #uuid "4849233c-e677-553e-b525-8ce1ed84e89f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424203} {:answer/id #uuid "6e36ebd6-f283-5b52-b5a9-f667a2f4b389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424209} {:answer/id #uuid "cd60ac0d-9b78-5c69-bd7b-06d4c14bdb66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424219} {:answer/id #uuid "f47d0d8f-c2bd-587b-8969-e8a2b85184c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4849233c-e677-553e-b525-8ce1ed84e89f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424203} :answer-field/id #uuid "3452daaf-b0f7-51b4-a94c-d00ee7b3816f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424221}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df216990-7a71-5841-8a5b-955dc6939d8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-123196" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=(x+y)^{2}\\,i+(y+z)^{2}\\,j+(x+z)^{2}\\,k.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y+z)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x+z)^{2}) \\\\  & =2(x+y)+2(y+z)+2(x+z) \\\\  & =4(x+y+z).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,1,-1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(-2,1,-1) & =4((-2)+(1)+(-1)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Three Dimensions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133890 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133706 :question/id #uuid "3f724b05-571d-529d-ab2b-db9dd6d2b531" :question/math-academy-id "e-9502" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)= xe^{y}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $x + 2y=3$ for $x\\in [-1,1],$ evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\dfrac{1}{2}(3-t)\\right\\rangle, \\quad t\\in[-1,1].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨-1,2⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(-1,2)$ and $(1,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(-1)) \\\\  & =f(1,1)-f(-1,2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot {e}^{1}-(-1)\\cdot {e}^{2} \\\\  & =e+{e}^{2} \\\\  & =e(1+e).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048645 :topic/math-academy-id 2635 :topic/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Paths Expressed as Functions of X"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049633 :topic/math-academy-id 3689 :topic/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Paths Expressed as Functions of Y"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134080 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5e9a404-efe6-5b3b-a968-bfdfa07d2715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258360} {:answer/id #uuid "d5693fb5-1c95-5d53-98c5-dad3ec95b36e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258653} {:answer/id #uuid "e2019a38-971e-5b2a-9a5d-ae0c0aa16381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258745} {:answer/id #uuid "fae96c7d-10ac-5879-af4d-db7bcd382a9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258979} {:answer/id #uuid "fec97874-d782-5e29-a6f7-4e842edeb489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fec97874-d782-5e29-a6f7-4e842edeb489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259019} :answer-field/id #uuid "ade72e6c-8ec1-553b-996a-8de4759ef4fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259336}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1d55b2c-030e-51c0-add3-e51bc195b461" :question/math-academy-id "q-123879" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve ${y}^{2}=4x$ for $y\\in [0,2\\sqrt{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\sqrt{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(2\\sqrt{2}) & =⟨2,2\\sqrt{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2\\sqrt{2}))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(2,2\\sqrt{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =((2\\sqrt{2})^{2}-{2}^{2})-({0}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =(8-4)-(0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41011d94-bc36-5377-837a-407beee2c7d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{5}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324496} {:answer/id #uuid "cf9dda28-e794-5e5b-a487-30e79ca01306" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}({\\pi }^{3}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325775} {:answer/id #uuid "d96c59c5-5422-5219-a8e3-f9082dc413ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi ({\\pi }^{3}-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325864} {:answer/id #uuid "d9734389-fc6e-5cc3-bc9b-423f0f252b71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}({\\pi }^{2}-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325866} {:answer/id #uuid "d9cab25b-2879-5d7f-974c-d9ca4022e0ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}({\\pi }^{3}-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9cab25b-2879-5d7f-974c-d9ca4022e0ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}({\\pi }^{3}-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325868} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bbf6d50-6f3e-5cf9-904b-81299d3aca6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326272}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "119e34da-cc6f-596d-82b2-8381c511738d" :question/math-academy-id "q-39749" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{3}-2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{t}^{3}-2⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-1⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-1)$ and $(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}\\cdot ({\\pi }^{3}-3)-{0}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}({\\pi }^{3}-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1adb26c2-291f-5ecb-be27-a83497de5b74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238279} {:answer/id #uuid "a4933581-9ab8-5174-ab43-a4a7af154ea9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239482} {:answer/id #uuid "add0b6a3-0052-52a8-a4b1-57278ac77df3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239563} {:answer/id #uuid "b0815d5d-2eab-5eb6-b0b6-cea74e67c474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239588} {:answer/id #uuid "bc186f6a-3c28-5f17-b90f-9614a603b361" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4933581-9ab8-5174-ab43-a4a7af154ea9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239482} :answer-field/id #uuid "46f03b38-7ac7-5b2a-bebf-d97ba46b022c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240454}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b025c1d-a393-5ea5-8c4e-6e0786f6f25d" :question/math-academy-id "q-104617" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2x$ for  $x\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2\\pi )-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =(0+1)-(0+1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "324fc058-3716-52e2-915c-a8ff8548ce66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238492} {:answer/id #uuid "40f10544-0cd1-50c6-9897-c035b59965c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238616} {:answer/id #uuid "99009bfa-183e-50ad-b31d-4a8cd8b1b4fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239384} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9e232f-a132-5ae1-a985-ee43b1e859ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239575} {:answer/id #uuid "b4578f1a-d647-5132-b9c6-b097afd1fc07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4578f1a-d647-5132-b9c6-b097afd1fc07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239632} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc00398-8a1c-5911-a2b1-71601fb149ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240344}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45e3e133-a99c-58ca-b74f-4cbfe235bb72" :question/math-academy-id "q-104625" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}).$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{3}+1$ for $x\\in [1,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{3}+1⟩,\\,t\\in [1,2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,2)$ and $(2,9).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f(2,9)-f(1,2) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})-{e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot 1-{e}^{1}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240908 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cb86dc4-a7cd-5ca1-b598-954b892dcccd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "171" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238168} {:answer/id #uuid "23431fc6-f04f-5360-af6d-30876ddc94bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "146" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238365} {:answer/id #uuid "8bcde37b-9a32-506b-bf91-9dcabb76716e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "198" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239270} {:answer/id #uuid "9b44e22a-7576-5afa-b3b8-4a77db65eb5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239409} {:answer/id #uuid "a90ce1ba-c1cf-56f7-b6bb-6a5a1b7fdca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "213" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cb86dc4-a7cd-5ca1-b598-954b892dcccd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "171" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238168} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ae2dfe3-e747-5345-997b-435580cc53ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240602}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "893763e7-56bb-5bf7-9f2b-99426982714b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104626" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}).$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [-1,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in [-1,2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨-1,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(-1,2)$ and $(2,8).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(-1)) \\\\  & =f(2,8)-f(-1,2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3}+{8}^{3})-\\frac{1}{3}((-1)^{3}+{2}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(520)-\\frac{1}{3}(7) \\\\  & =\\frac{520}{3}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =171.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259588 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d21652b-8d10-5c45-b44f-db44c924244e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257022} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad034e3-435a-54af-8b44-ed2fc0bf168f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257573} {:answer/id #uuid "863b68a3-5559-5883-8119-c9324587def7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257962} {:answer/id #uuid "a621cce8-352a-549d-ba06-938de99fba53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258228} {:answer/id #uuid "edd3b1c2-37e9-5a98-86ec-a93068bfe7d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ad034e3-435a-54af-8b44-ed2fc0bf168f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257573} :answer-field/id #uuid "27cdb6ef-9816-548d-bfcd-7bdee6ce6dbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259102}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "530f83ac-873a-5207-ba3d-e9438dabfd29" :question/math-academy-id "q-124027" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y+1}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y={e}^{x}+1$ for $x\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{e}^{t}+1⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,2⟩, \\\\ r(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,2)$ and $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,3)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {e}^{3+1}-0\\cdot {e}^{2+1} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259589 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c9589f-c628-5f4a-a78a-70eef556a95c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256839} {:answer/id #uuid "7d69fec3-73fc-5815-8bc3-797953319185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257889} {:answer/id #uuid "8020ab34-3c45-5159-8e7f-6c5ce658702c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257922} {:answer/id #uuid "da2ca4d2-0f0c-5594-9b9e-6e91ba3983b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258687} {:answer/id #uuid "e253a7c1-631c-5b5e-ba82-916317b443b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d69fec3-73fc-5815-8bc3-797953319185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257889} :answer-field/id #uuid "33c80ca6-5207-5a6c-a174-ad7e192a275e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259129}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "388c57e6-e07f-5b15-a475-f9e96568702d" :question/math-academy-id "q-124028" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{3}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for $x\\in [1,{e}^{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,{e}^{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r({e}^{2}) & =⟨{e}^{2},2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $({e}^{2},2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r({e}^{2}))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f({e}^{2},2)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {({e}^{2})}^{3}-0\\cdot {1}^{3} \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}-0 \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259590 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0794e38d-83c6-592a-8698-603e9bbcd5de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256847} {:answer/id #uuid "239089df-2146-51b4-b115-a88f3d39c9c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257080} {:answer/id #uuid "79dbb24e-2d02-5659-8575-c9dca1acb841" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257858} {:answer/id #uuid "8e3ff693-01df-587b-b8f8-2302b9d2cfa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258036} {:answer/id #uuid "bd179ab0-00b3-5bab-8759-a6525529b5da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd179ab0-00b3-5bab-8759-a6525529b5da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258432} :answer-field/id #uuid "7766e048-d9fa-5296-ac66-21288dbdd49b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259233}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e457dc8-8930-562f-905f-f48a2ebaba17" :question/math-academy-id "q-124029" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve ${y}^{2}=x$ for $y\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259591 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5570ad04-a911-54fe-882f-cae6a0eccb35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-{e}^{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257514} {:answer/id #uuid "69d52aa2-f5a3-518e-9e37-8487d0ca7564" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257719} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdee35c-5f2c-5453-8ea0-524f7e2d5558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{16}-{e}^{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257879} {:answer/id #uuid "a641ae1f-fce3-54ae-b9d3-8c7c4dd0c256" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{16}+{e}^{4}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258233} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f247a0-2670-5ca0-8d3b-f9534636b266" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{16}+16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bdee35c-5f2c-5453-8ea0-524f7e2d5558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{16}-{e}^{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257879} :answer-field/id #uuid "399493dc-69ea-5245-b7ef-1fc5d184a6d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259138}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33d927c3-e1a0-5a24-bd3b-65369e4fc02e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124030" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{e}^{xy}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\frac{x}{2}=\\sqrt{y}$ for $y\\in [1,4],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\sqrt{t},t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,4].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2,1⟩, \\\\ r(4) & =⟨4,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(4))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f(4,4)-f(2,1) \\\\  & =({4}^{2}+{e}^{4\\cdot 4})-({2}^{2}+{e}^{2\\cdot 1}) \\\\  & =(16+{e}^{16})-(4+{e}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{16}-{e}^{2}+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259592 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "572cddd3-8f84-5b08-8f30-ba3696a9b1fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{\\pi }-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257535} {:answer/id #uuid "980d5c51-5b40-5f56-bfb7-99d5b6db0c42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\pi }-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258122} {:answer/id #uuid "ba90b885-3030-535b-a6d6-b20cfce89c1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258416} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f40706-748a-5462-8a78-9770b7b5dbdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{\\pi }}{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258776} {:answer/id #uuid "f1fcb8ed-37e1-5237-bf6c-95c7528a9627" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{\\pi }}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba90b885-3030-535b-a6d6-b20cfce89c1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258416} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdaa45ba-790a-5b06-b275-79f77436acf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259360}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b4571c4-6fd7-5557-9855-d69404faa890" :question/math-academy-id "q-124032" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2x$ for $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{t}{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{e}^{0}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259593 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "228757f4-13cc-55fe-9095-a48e100ceb16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257067} {:answer/id #uuid "2a73a14f-a5c6-5495-a6bd-6839c844573f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257158} {:answer/id #uuid "75872a6e-2056-5f4d-a136-119cb8679c55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257812} {:answer/id #uuid "c2078454-b9ed-517e-8d5c-46c93d61beb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258484} {:answer/id #uuid "fdbba96b-984e-5d92-80e9-dd02e8dff0a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdbba96b-984e-5d92-80e9-dd02e8dff0a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259001} :answer-field/id #uuid "db56c118-70b4-55be-8655-9ad9749d2c6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259417}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc34530a-2e34-53d7-8fcc-b9ff1814815f" :question/math-academy-id "q-124034" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $2x=\\pi {y}^{3}$ for $y\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{\\pi {t}^{3}}{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡1-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259594 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47b3eeba-4eac-5d44-9adc-3211c30eb7ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257409} {:answer/id #uuid "5a534628-6707-5097-afac-65143ed14073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257569} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad476ab-38fd-5e4e-8d0a-5341b7b7b7a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257574} {:answer/id #uuid "7dec0392-9c5a-5bf9-bc94-0852a51e8494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257896} {:answer/id #uuid "d412fdac-65be-56fb-9312-852052cc11df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a534628-6707-5097-afac-65143ed14073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257569} :answer-field/id #uuid "19ade967-0b40-518f-b3ab-2d00cfb750fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259075}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c2ccfca-31f7-5fd8-9901-48e158e0be78" :question/math-academy-id "q-124035" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ for $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨-1,\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(-1,\\pi )-f(1,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}-(-1))-({0}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}+1)+1 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}]}
